WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.076 --> 00:00:01.260
(mysterious dreamy dance music)

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:02.700
- [Narrator] Located in Madagascar

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.970
is a forest unlike any other.

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:08.573
Because if you look closely,
this forest is made of stone.

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:15.890
The Tsingy de Bemaraha, also
known as Tsingy Stone Forest,

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.960
is made of limestone,
which was once underwater

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.193
some 200 million years ago.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:26.300
The rocks all feature distinct,
razor-sharp formations,

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.700
created by erosion from tropical rain,

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.270
which washed away the softer stone.

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:34.820
The reserve is around
230 square miles in size,

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:38.823
and the stone towers rise
220 feet from the ground.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.090
Although this dramatic landscape

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.410
appears inhospitable to nature,

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:49.610
Tsingy is home to some
very resilient wildlife,

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:52.430
including numerous wild bird populations

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:54.893
and 11 different species of lemur.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.050
The word Tsingy is indigenous
to the Malagasy language

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:04.710
and aptly translates to where
one cannot walk barefoot.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:07.370
However, while one cannot walk barefoot,

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:09.400
the Tsingy Stone Forest is accessible

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.533
to those wanting to see and
climb the forest in real life.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.557
(sonic beeping)

